JavaScript Semicolon (;) and Automatic Semicolon Insertion (ASI)
---------------------------------------------------------------

-> A semicolon (;) is used to mark the end of a JavaScript statement.

-> Unlike languages such as C++ and Java, where semicolons are mandatory, JavaScript allows semicolons to be omitted in most cases.

-> This is possible because JavaScript uses a mechanism called Automatic Semicolon Insertion (ASI).

-> ASI automatically inserts semicolons in certain situations when they are omitted.

-> The primary purpose of a semicolon is to tell the JavaScript engine where one statement ends and the next begins.

-> ASI works best when statements are written on separate lines.

Example:

let name = "John"
console.log(name)

JavaScript interprets it as:

let name = "John";
console.log(name);

-> If multiple statements are written on the same line, semicolons are required to separate them.

Incorrect:

alert("First") alert("Second")

Correct:

alert("First"); alert("Second");

-> Although semicolons are optional in many cases, using them explicitly is considered a good practice.

Benefits:
   - Improves readability.
   - Clearly separates statements.
   - Prevents unexpected behavior caused by ASI.
   - Makes code more consistent.